A||||22||fiero56||Windows ME, worth it?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||04-08-2001||09:18 PM||||Is ME worth buying? I've got win98, and just wondering if ME is worth the cost. Any particular pros/cons between the two?||||reg||5
Z||000001||p4n1c||04-08-2001||09:39 PM||||My recommendation is not to waste your money on that worthless pile of crap.  I test operating systems when they come out and ME did not stand up to Win98 or 98 SE.  I had 98 with all the patches from the Windows Update site for more then half a year and it worked flawlessly, I upgraded to ME to try it out and it lasted for about a month.  I'm running 2000 Pro now.  I'd wait for XP to come out. <P>Or better yet switch to Linux or BeOS 5 Pro(to bad hardly any applications are out for it).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 04-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||04-08-2001||09:45 PM||||I upgraded from W95 to ME and it was well worth it. A very nice system on my machine.<BR>Saw a comparison in a magazine awhile back between W98 and ME, and their opinion was that it wasn't worthwhile to "upgrade" to ME. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jaygee79||04-09-2001||12:00 AM||||I have ME and I like it.  I would recommend it.  I really don't notice much difference in the 2, to tell you the truth.  There are differences, but nothing absolutely major.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Tweek||04-09-2001||12:24 AM||||XP?? youve got to be kidding. As a tech who works for a completely unrelated part of microsoft what Ive heard is that that OS will require a constant connection to the internet. You want to use mspaint, word? got to d/l for every use. plus there is talk of having to pay a monthly fee for it. IMHO it will be the downfall of microsoft. For now I'll stick to 98. When more apps be be supported on it maybe i'll go to linux or some other non-ms OS.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||04-09-2001||12:36 AM||||If you do a lot of digital pic's (camera or very high resolution scanner) Windows ME is not a good thing to go to. (ME = More Errors)<BR>You would need to seek updated drivers or custom drivers for the equipment you have. If you are using a windows driver for a color printer you would need to get the updated version from the printer manufacturer.  Usually it's a lot of little "buggy" problems with drivers and libraries. I know a few who have upgraded and only had 1 or 2 issues to correct and the machine has worked flawlessly. Others had to remove it because they could never get it to work right. It's a flip of the coin as to if it's good or bad. If your present windows is working without any crashes or bugs, you could try it. If you have occasional crashes and such, don't upgrade, the crashes seem to get worse.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||04-09-2001||12:39 AM||||My IBM Aptiva came with Windows 98SE on it. I upgraded to Windows 2000 Pro a few months before it was available to the public. I loves the speed and efficiency of it, but I lost use of several of my peripherals including my scanner and my TV card. So I looked for something else to use while they got 2000 more compatible.<P>I reformatted my HD again and went to Windows ME. I was immediately sorry that I had. It is so slow and freezes all of the time. Instead of going through all of that hassle again to go back to 98SE, I used it for about 6 months. I would never recommend it to anyone! If you don't want of need 2000 Pro, stay with 98SE. You'll be much happier! Just get all of the service packs that Microsoft puts out...<P>I'm back on 2000 Pro since they started releasing Service Packs and compatibility upgrades and I couldn't be happier. It's a great operating system in spite of what critics say...<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||pHoOl||04-09-2001||03:15 AM||||I've been on windows 2000 professional since before it was released to the public, and let me tell you, it is the best Microsoft OS i have ever had.  Doesn't crash at all.  Even if the shell crashes, it still stays alive, and restarts it automatically.  you never have to turn the computer off.  <P>And don't think that you have to have a new machine to run it either.  i was running it on a pentium 75mHz.  (6 year old computer).<P> Also, windows 2000 is much more secure than 98's standard setup.  Granted, you can fix this in 98, but it takes a lot of digging that the average computer user won't know how to do.<P>okay, that's my pitch.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||04-09-2001||08:41 AM||||My vote goes to Win98SE for regular home users, or Win2000 Pro for more power users.  The only reason I'm not running 2000 at home is it doesn't support all my games.  Other than that, I'd be running it.  WinME isn't worth the effort, IMO.  BTW, I'm a computer analyst by trade, and I've never seen ME successfully deployed in the office.  If they don't want a version of NT, then it's Win95 or 98.  ME isn't even considered.||||reg||13
Z||000009||JSocha||04-09-2001||11:17 AM||||At work we run everything from Win 98 SE (all patches), a few Windows NT v4.0 workstations (w/SP6) and a laptop with W2000 Pro and now one workstation we converted from NT Workstation (since it crashed and I didn't feel like repartionitioning the HD to with Partion Magic just to use the full drive instead of dividing it up to a "C" and "D" drive).<P>If it wasn't for the fact that there are still not a lot of drivers available for Windows 2000 Pro and since our Manufacturing/Accounting software is not compatiable with the version of that software we are currently running now, I would have converted all our workstations to 2K Pro months ago for the stability and reliability over that of any previous version of Windows.<P>I myself would recommend 2K Professional to anyone thinking on upgrading.  Recovers from a crash a lot better (if it ever does crash as I have only had it happen once due to the fact the battery on the laptop drained before I could shut it down properly) and it recovered just B-U-T-FULL.<P>Forgo ME is not only my opinion, but my recomendation.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cliff Pennock||04-09-2001||12:04 PM||||I <B>love</B> WinMe! I've never made so much money solving computer problems before!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||04-09-2001||12:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B><BR>I myself would recommend 2K Professional to anyone thinking on upgrading.  Recovers from a crash a lot better (if it ever does crash as I have only had it happen once due to the fact the battery on the laptop drained before I could shut it down properly) and it recovered just B-U-T-FULL.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll second this. <BR>Due to hardware difficulties with my 'puter ("stock" BP6 voltage regulators don't play well with Nvidia AGP cards), my machine got to the point that it would freeze on a regular basis. Only remedy was a push of the reset button. It consistantly restarted with no difficulties. After a while, I FINALLY hosed Word so badly that it would generate an error and shut down. (Word. Not 2K.) <BR>Had to reinstall W2K to fix the problem.<BR>I simply loaded the disk and ran the setup. No reformat. No nothing.<BR>It remembered all of my settings and all of my tweeks. Was truly painless. <BR>I've never been that lucky with NT.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-09-2001||07:57 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.winmag.com" TARGET=_blank>www.winmag.com</A>  in just about every review they hate it.<P>WindozeXp.... Xperience the biggest screw job yet. The new "copy protection" system alone will be an absolute nightmare. Can We say Lotus 123 With Superlock? License management is hard enough without this new bind it to the hardware mess.<P>Microsofts answer to Email viruses... permanently disable the ability to download at least 30 file types. This will be built into the new office and can't be turned off w/o registry hacking. maybe not even then.... ||||reg||1
Z||000013||sjp777||04-09-2001||11:01 PM||||Win ME is great if you do a clean install, have relatively current hardware, and a PIII or Celeron at at least 600MHZ and at least 128mb of ram. If not, stick with 98.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Songman||04-10-2001||12:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sjp777:<BR><B>Win ME is great if you do a clean install, have relatively current hardware, and a PIII or Celeron at at least 600MHZ and at least 128mb of ram. If not, stick with 98.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Glad you had luck with it.. I got all that, and did a clean install, and 20 years of professional computer experience behind me, and I still hated it... heheh<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||04-10-2001||06:42 AM||||Drew--Stick with 98SE.  If you remember, I upgraded my old computer from 98 to ME.  It kept crashing.  I finally lost almost everything I had spent so long saving (including most of a novel I had worked on for years).  When I bought my new computer, I asked for Win98SE.  Gateway (liars) told me that either I got ME or nothing at all.  So far I haven't had any problems with ME on the new setup, but...  I'm not a computer brain like some on here, but I'd still say to stick with what you've got.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000016||JSocha||04-10-2001||09:23 AM||||Out of Curiosity?<P>Haven't kept up much with MS News, however, within the last year MS was at one time considering on having computer stores like DELL, Gateway(yuck), Micron, etc. right down to your local computer store have a master copy of the WIN OS where they would install it and you would not give you a copy of the OS.<P>If your computer crashed or you needed to reinstall the OS, you would have to pay for it or send your computer back so they could do.<P>Does anyone know if that died at MS?  Or is it still a massive "Brain F*rt" that they may throw on to us sometime soon as some of MS's current plans with how their next OS release is going to handle things seems to be closer then we think?<P>And has any one figured out that message while doing an online Windows Update:<P>Please Wait...Windows Update is customizing the product updates catalog for your computer. "This is done without sending any information to Microsoft."<P>Well then, if they aren't sending information back to MS, how do they know when your little old puter is in need of a "critical update" when you go online (other then shutting off the Windows Update option feature in MSCONFIG)?<P>Does anyone else buy that statement?  Seems like a lot of other computer professionals and/or consultants do not!<P>Anyways, my recommendation if your going to or need to upgrade to a more recent and more stable OS is to do a clean install of W2K Pro providing you have more then 32+MB of RAM on your old puter.  W2K-Pro will not install on a PC with less then this.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero5a_3.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000017||TheFiero64||04-10-2001||10:48 AM||||I love my windows ME. I have not had one bit of trouble with it. I also did a clean install. <P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000018||Songman||04-10-2001||12:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B>And has any one figured out that message while doing an online Windows Update:<P>Please Wait...Windows Update is customizing the product updates catalog for your computer. "This is done without sending any information to Microsoft."<P>Well then, if they aren't sending information back to MS, how do they know when your little old puter is in need of a "critical update" when you go online (other then shutting off the Windows Update option feature in MSCONFIG)?<P>Does anyone else buy that statement?  Seems like a lot of other computer professionals and/or consultants do not!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yeah, I've thought about that many times. Of course, you are sending information to them. You send information everytime you visit a website, muchless this. You are exactly right, they wouldn't know what you needed if they didn't get info from you.<P>But I don't really care all that much. If they want to know how much bootleg software I have on my computer, they can just go ahead and look! hehe<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||olympic||04-11-2001||06:10 AM||||Oh boy, I just ordered 2 AMD Duron 800 MHZ computers.  One for home and one for my office.  THey have 128MB ram and 60 GB HD's.  Both will come with WinME.  I really hope I dont have any problems with the OS!<BR>  <BR>   My current computer is a P1 100MHZ running Win98 with all the patches.  I've had it for about 3 years and I had to reformat the HD once about 18 months ago and I need to do it again before I sell it.  The OS seems to get more unstable as the months go by and the computer locks up more often.  Eventually locking up every time I log off the net or when the computer comes off of stand by.  So I just back up all the important stuff, wipe the C drive and reinstall win98 and download the patches and plug-ins.  Works like a charm.<P>  I still have my copy of Win98 so I guess if I'm really unhappy with  WinME on my new computers I can just reformat the HD's and load Win98.||||reg||1
Z||000020||fierofreek2001||04-12-2001||10:25 PM||||i dont see any probs with winME, im sure if you have all the windows 98 patches tho, it wouldnt really be necessary to buy it... the only thing i dont like about winME is that you cant restart the comp in DOS... kinda sucks... but whatever<BR>its fine for just home everyday use <BR>steve||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cheever2||04-12-2001||10:46 PM||||For almost 6 months I've had no problems from my Compaq from Best Buy with AMD @ 900 Mhz on 128 ram w/ 30 gig HD.  I didn't know it had ME until I got it home & turned it on, and had never heard of it before.  But so far so good.  Been the best home PC I've had (this is my 3rd).<P>||||reg||6
Z||000022||coon420dog||11-11-2001||02:08 PM||||NO SOFTWARE IS WORTH IT!  Windows 2000 / XP / NT work well but they do have bugs and they are over-rated.  I have had XP for 2 months now and I am already sick of the problems that it has.  I would suggest moving over to UNIX / linux.  Any software that you will ever need is free!  It takes a while to get comfortable with UNIX but it is well worth the effort||||reg||1
